[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `SIGPIPE` was ignored in Rust but fatal in C — **real defect, fixed**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The only behavioural divergence found in the translation.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Symptom.** With nothing left reading stdout, the C program is killed by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `SIGPIPE` when it finally calls `printf` (wait status 141, `code = None`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `signal = Some(13)`), while the Rust program exited 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m       $ ./sp_c   | true   # C stand-in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m       PIPESTATUS[0]=141
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m       $ ./sp_rs  | true   # Rust stand-in, before the fix
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m       PIPESTATUS[0]=0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Cause.** The Rust runtime installs `SIG_IGN` for `SIGPIPE` before `main`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   runs. A C program starts with the default disposition, so the failing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `write` terminates it instead of returning `EPIPE`. The translation ignored
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the `write!` result (correctly mirroring C's unchecked `printf`), so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   ignored signal turned a fatal condition into a silent success.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Fix.** `restore_default_sigpipe()` in `translation/src/main.rs`, called as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the first statement of `main`, sets `SIGPIPE` back to `SIG_DFL`. Verified
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   with stand-ins: unfixed Rust → `(Some(0), None)`, fixed Rust → `(None,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Some(13))`, C → `(None, Some(13))`. Normal runs (reader present) are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   unaffected and still exit 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Regression test.** `full_run::stdout_reader_gone_kills_both_the_same_way`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Only the accepting path writes to stdout, so this test pays a full workload
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   run.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Test-harness defects found (not translation defects)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Recorded because both initially showed up as red tests and could be
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m misread as translation problems.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. A 1 MiB `argv[1]` cannot be exec'd at all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `invalid_seed_very_long_argument` first used `vec![b'7'; 1024 * 1024]` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m failed with `Argument list too long (os error 7)` — for *both* binaries. Linux
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m caps a single argv entry at `MAX_ARG_STRLEN` (128 KiB), so neither program can
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m be started, and the case measures the kernel rather than the translation.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reduced to 100 000 digits, which still exercises `ERANGE` plus a very large
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m error message.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 3. The `SIGPIPE` test leaked the pipe's read end into the child
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The first version of the regression test above used `pipe(2)`, whose fds are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m not close-on-exec. The child therefore *inherited* the read end, kept the pipe
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m alive, wrote successfully and exited 0 — so the test failed asserting that the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **C** program should have died from `SIGPIPE`. Switched to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `pipe2(fds, O_CLOEXEC)`; the write end survives because the spawn machinery
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `dup2`s it onto fd 1 (which clears `O_CLOEXEC`), while the read end is closed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m by `exec`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
